The "target draws on the floor" finding was incomplete: the TARGET is rendered
as a HOLLOW OUTLINE "ghost" sprite (the object is the SOLID version of the same
sprite). arc3_frame_induction.induce_object_target_attrs reads all five
attributes of both, frame-only, zero internal state:
  - position = the sprite box (object: filled bbox; target: outline centroid
    with a notch-bias correction -nub_vector*scale)
  - scale    = box size / 4 (4x4 = scale 1, 8x8 = scale 2)
  - property = the sprite COLOUR (color_remap sets object colour = property)
  - rotation = the 2-cell directional NOTCH edge, calibrated vs internal truth
    (bottom=0, left=90, top=180, right=270 — clockwise)

Validated EXACT + end-to-end: frame-induced (x,y,scale,rotation,property) ==
internal truth for object AND target on tn36 L1-L5, and feeding the frame-induced
object+target into arc_program_editor_model.plan_program then running the plan in
the REAL ENV WINS 5/5 (incl. L4 scale<->collision, L5 rotation+scale+property).
Zero internal state on the perception+planning path.

This CLOSES the chain for the program-editor transform class:
DETECT -> ROUTE -> PERCEIVE (object+target attrs, frame-only) -> PLAN (offline
transition model) -> env-confirmed SOLVE. A complete frame-only, internal-state-
free live solve, demonstrated. Residual: the maze sub-fields (checkpoints draw on
floor, at-rest hazards invisible) for L6/L7 routing.

